      "writtenOn": "2017-07-26T13:28:25Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "An enum class can totally implement Comparable, but the syntax for the user is kind of crappy ... foo.compareTo(bar) \u003c 0 plus I need to define compareTo(). Unfortunately implementing Comparable does not allow you to use the \u003c, \u003c\u003d, \u003e, \u003e\u003d operators.\n\nI could put the priority values on the enum as I do with the TRON event id, but then I need to map the eventId I keep in the map to a NotificationType, and this gets ugly because I would need to return something by default when it is not in the map, and I can\u0027t use null if I try to access a priority field on the NotificationType objects.\n\nThat imo works better if I have native ints, but not well if I have an enum type.",
      "parentUuid": "d3f4b3a7_108d4a57",
      "revId": "33b49b4a144b6c5519ccd8a98cb8ae2bd6238e51",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
